This Chapter is about Linux file systems, directories, paths, and basic file management.

The Linux root directory /
/ is the top-level directory in Linux.
All other directories are located underneath it.
Example: /
├── home
├── etc
├── var
├── tmp
└── root
Home directories
/home contains users' home directories.
For example: /home/bob
~ represents your own home directory.
~bob represents Bob's home directory when used with cd.
cd ~bob
Absolute and relative paths
Absolute path starts from /: 
/etc/ppp
/home/bob
Relative path starts from your current location: 
documents/file.txt
Navigating directories with cd
cd means change directory.
cd /etc
Go to /etc
cd ..
Go up one directory.
cd ~
Listing files with ls
ls
Lists files and directories.
ls -a
Hidden files
Linux hidden files normally begin with a dot ..
Example:
.hidden
.config
Creating and managing files
I learned commands such as: touch file.txt
Creates a new empty file or updates its timestamp.
Renames a file. -mv old.txt new.txt
Copies a file. -cp file.txt backup.txt
Removes a file.- rm file.txt
The touch command can:
Create new files
Update the timestamp of existing files
Wildcards / glob characters
You learned three important glob characters:
Character	Meaning
*	Zero or more characters
?	Exactly one character
[ ]	Matches characters inside the brackets
For example: ls *.txt  -finds files ending in .txt.

Chapter 7 is being able to navigate the Linux file system and identify, create, rename, copy, remove and search for files using paths and wildcards
